    "narrative": "For July 22, the analytical engine highlights **Tyler Soderstrom** (76.91) as our premier offensive weapon, buoyed by a favorable matchup against Meibrys Kelly (5.31 xERA) and a prime spot in the two-hole. **James Wood** (71.14) and **Mickey Moniak** (69.64) follow closely, both benefitting from double-digit park factors that elevate their already strong base projections. **Vinnie Pasquantino** (67.14) represents our most significant platoon-driven ceiling, as his matchup against Roupp allows for a +14% dynamic platoon boost that overcomes the slight negative pressure from the wind. While **Corbin Carroll** (59.77) lands just outside the 60+ tier, his inclusion remains mandatory; we accept the slightly depressed efficiency due to the L/L matchup because his elite stolen base potential is critical to closing the gap in our scarcest category.\n\nThe most difficult personnel decision today involved **Josh Naylor**, who sits on the bench despite a respectable 59.86 score. While he remains a strong asset, his efficiency was capped by a poor historical BvP profile against Singer and a negative park factor. He was ultimately edged out by **Austin Riley** (64.43), whose matchup against Michael King provides superior projected run-scoring potential. We are monitoring the weather closely regarding **Vinnie Pasquantino** and **Seiya Suzuki**, as incoming winds are exerting downward pressure on their scoring profiles. We have cleared our bench of **Edwin Arroyo** (38.73), as he fell below our \"Below Floor\" threshold, keeping our limited game caps preserved for the higher-output starts like **Isaac Paredes** (65.35) and **Jose Caballero** (61.07), the latter of whom gains substantial value from a soft matchup against Mitch Keller.",
